WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.340
But when I get asked that question
again, what's the best way to go

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.240
about learning a new language?

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.560
You know, what do you
do in your first month?

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:04.800
Right.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.470
I often tell people that you can
do a lot worse than just grab a

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:08.970
textbook and just flick through it.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.000
You know, just read...

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.600
just quickly look at the grammar points.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:13.380
Don't do any of it.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.890
Don't start studying, but just look
through, see what the grammar points are.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:22.090
Put it on the map because once you've
got that, um, general awareness of the

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.460
kind of key rocks grammaticaly, the
rocks of meaning if you like, the way

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.720
meaning is expressed in the language.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.630
Once you have an awareness
of that territory, then that,

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.439
that really does I think...

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:33.540
it is very helpful.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.360
And then you just put flesh on
the bone after that, as you, as

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.030
you carry on and, and, um, and,
and actually consume content.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.580
And that tends to sort
of repeat that pattern.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.320
In other words, there's this Sufi saying,
you can only learn what you already know.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.860
And we all had the experience that
we don't notice anything until all

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.920
of a sudden something is pointed out
to us and we notice it everywhere.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.720
And so to that extent, I think
some initial overview of the

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.750
grammar helps us notice things
that we might otherwise not notice.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.720
Uh, It's not enough.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.460
So I do think as I...

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:13.740
getting back to my earlier point, that
I would like to see, uh, audio books

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.030
of say Portuguese grammar or Japanese
grammar or whatever it might be with lots

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.220
of examples, no quizzes, no exercises.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.370
I am totally against any
form of quiz or exercise.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.260
It's all about exposure, but when
a certain concept is introduced,

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:29.590
then there should be 10 examples.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.780
So we get a concentrated dose
of whatever, you know, two

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.070
forms of past tense or whatever.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.490
It might be an explanation,
10 examples, all in the target

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.260
language, all with audio.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:44.370
And you can listen to that and
it's divided up into, uh, you know,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:49.259
chapters, verbs, nouns, whatever it
might be, adjectives, conjunctions,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.190
and you can be listening to this sort
of explanation of the language in the

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.350
target language with lots of examples.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.720
So it's meaningful by
that time you're curious.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.039
Now you want to get better at this.

00:01:59.804 --> 00:02:01.365
But you want it in the target language.

00:02:01.395 --> 00:02:02.595
It's meaningful input.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:03.675
It's meaningful to me.

00:02:03.945 --> 00:02:04.575
I listen to it.

00:02:05.025 --> 00:02:05.685
It's just a thought.

00:02:05.685 --> 00:02:09.195
I think that the grammar right now
is sort of focused on trying to

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:12.045
teach the grammar to people who don't
yet have much experience with the

00:02:12.045 --> 00:02:16.185
language and expecting them then to
write this test and get it right.

00:02:16.995 --> 00:02:19.635
My view is until they've had enough
experience with the language,

00:02:19.635 --> 00:02:20.655
they're not going to get it right.

00:02:20.655 --> 00:02:23.075
Or they might get it right and then
get it wrong and then get it right.

00:02:23.725 --> 00:02:26.880
Uh, it's not by doing exercises
that they're going to be better.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:32.370
On the other hand, someone who is
already at this B1 B2 level, give

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.850
them something that they can treat
as meaningful input, comprehensible

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:40.800
input, and sort of start to refine
their, their accuracy in the language.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.485
And that's why we always get these
examples of students who will say,

00:02:46.485 --> 00:02:49.815
you know, why is it that's I, uh, I
always, whenever I'm talking with my

00:02:49.815 --> 00:02:54.345
tutor in a, in a lesson or I'd never
have any problem and I can always get

00:02:54.345 --> 00:02:57.915
the answers right in my textbook, but
then when I'm out on my own, it's like

00:02:57.915 --> 00:03:02.235
I freeze up and I, and I can't get
anything meaningful out of my mouth.

00:03:02.265 --> 00:03:05.250
It's it's it's for that,
it's for that exact reason.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:10.320
Um, so you were talking about, I guess
having a huge arsenal of examples.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.470
So you're, you're talking about having
the explanation and then just pages

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.630
of examples in the target language?

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.959
No, 10 is enough because let's
say there's 50 points or a hundred

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:20.000
points that you want to cover.

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:24.880
You're not going to cover every possible
situation in the language, but 50, or...

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.215
I bought a book once on Russian
called 52 Patterns in the

00:03:28.215 --> 00:03:29.275
Russian Language, it was great.

00:03:29.295 --> 00:03:30.285
That's how it was structured.

00:03:30.495 --> 00:03:34.255
And what would have been good there would
have been an audio book to acompany it.

00:03:34.875 --> 00:03:37.635
So there's X number of patterns in
the language that if you have these

00:03:37.635 --> 00:03:39.015
patterns, you can speak the language...

00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:43.125
Do you find that meaningful input for you?

00:03:43.155 --> 00:03:46.065
Because you described that as
input that's meaningful for you.

00:03:46.065 --> 00:03:51.149
And I'm curious about that because I I've
used programs like this in the past where

00:03:51.149 --> 00:03:54.329
it's just a huge numbers of examples
and sentences one after the other.

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:56.730
And I just get, I find myself
getting bored very quickly, precisely

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.260
because I don't find it meaningful.

00:03:58.649 --> 00:04:01.200
And it's one of those things
where, you know, this is the danger

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.239
between pedagogy and just content.

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:03.450
Isn't it?

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.609
Because, because with content, you, you
know, you're going to win in the end,

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.230
you know, you'll get there in the end.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.179
And then with the pedagogy
you've got a leg up.

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:18.089
You can explain it more, but
then how do you stay motivated?

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:19.920
It's got to find that balance in between.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:20.899
And I always...

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.450
Well, you know...

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:23.470
yeah.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.310
So, I mean, it's going to
be a part of your strategy.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:27.540
It's not the main thing.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:31.650
The main thing is listening to content
that is inherently more compelling,

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:37.620
but I can listen to my Persian podcasts
and there, there are patterns there

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:41.460
that, you know, I know what they mean
but I'm not sufficiently aware of them

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.850
so that when I speak with my person
tutor, I can use them correctly.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.890
And I have the feeling of...

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.770
certainly I try to focus in I'll...

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:54.090
I, I have a book in I'll go through
and see some of these grammar points.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.830
I have the feeling that it helps me
notice these things so that eventually

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:02.420
when I speak, I got them right more
often than I'm getting them right now.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.270
So, yeah, there's always the
danger, whatever you're doing you

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:07.500
don't know if it's doing any good.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:08.669
You're flipping through flashcards.

00:05:08.669 --> 00:05:09.570
Am I learning something?

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:10.109
I don't know.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:11.060
I'm reading the dictionary.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:11.820
Am I Learning something?

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:12.330
I don't know.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.440
We don't know, but I have the
feeling that I would listen to it.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.060
I would listen to it.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.539
And certainly the Russian book
helped me with my Russian patterns.

00:05:22.169 --> 00:05:24.479
Um, so it's just, it's a minor point.

00:05:24.479 --> 00:05:27.630
It's not the main, the main thing,
I agree with you, is still stories.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:29.050
But um...

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.590
Let me ask you this Steve, what have
you, what have you, you know, it's

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:35.190
been a couple of years since the,
since the pandemic now and I can,

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.640
I assume if it, can, I assume that
you've your language learning is

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.710
accelerated during the pandemic, or do
you, did you find yourself doing more?

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:43.400
Not Really?

00:05:43.599 --> 00:05:45.150
Um, no.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:50.550
I mean, uh, uh, I would say about the
same I put in my hour, hour and a half.

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:56.729
Um, you know, in the evenings or whatever,
I listen while I'm washing the dishes.

00:05:56.969 --> 00:05:58.140
I wouldn't say it's accelerated.

00:05:58.799 --> 00:06:01.140
What have you, what have you
learned in the last two years

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.609
about, about language learning?

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:06.450
What, is there anything that surprised
you or that has, that you've come to

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:08.039
believe in the last couple of years?

00:06:09.179 --> 00:06:14.190
Yeah, I think that, uh, I spend an awful
lot of time generating meaningful content.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:19.330
And so the, if to the extent that we
can make that easier for people uh,

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.724
we save ourselves a lot of time, the
amount of time that I go to YouTube and

00:06:22.724 --> 00:06:27.854
I put, you know, podcast on history and
Arabic, uh, put it in Arabic and look for

00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:29.594
something and don't find anything useful.

00:06:30.044 --> 00:06:34.995
Uh, so I th...no, that might be because
I'm into Arabic and Persian, but, uh,

00:06:35.055 --> 00:06:39.705
and then I look at, uh, you know, uh,
again, Sahra who lives in Iran, she's

00:06:39.705 --> 00:06:44.924
put together this sort of 26 part stories
on the history of Iran, each story then

00:06:44.924 --> 00:06:46.485
followed by these certain questions.

00:06:47.475 --> 00:06:49.305
Uh, phenomenal stuff.

00:06:49.335 --> 00:06:53.045
If I had something like that on the
history of Egypt, on the history of, um,

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:58.485
the Levant, you know, to me, content is
the big thing, making content better.

00:06:58.575 --> 00:07:02.475
Granted I've been in sort of languages
where there is less content available

00:07:02.805 --> 00:07:04.845
than say English, Spanish, whatever.

00:07:05.265 --> 00:07:11.295
So content, because fundamentally
it's still 80% compelling input,

00:07:11.755 --> 00:07:15.345
a certain amount of time spent
with the nuts and bolts of the

00:07:15.345 --> 00:07:16.455
language to help you notice that.

00:07:17.295 --> 00:07:19.455
And then when you have
the opportunity to use it.

00:07:20.025 --> 00:07:22.485
So I'm not a believer in
talking your way to fluency.

00:07:22.545 --> 00:07:25.605
I think you build up your potential
in the language, and then when you get

00:07:25.605 --> 00:07:29.025
the opportunity, you go for it without
worrying about the mistakes you can have.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.020
So none of that has changed.

00:07:31.049 --> 00:07:34.440
It's just that there's this
explosion of available content

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.270
and how do we best access it?

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:38.880
How do we make it easier
for people to find it?

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.440
That was a big part of what we did at
LingQ with our new 5.0 is to try, you

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:47.969
know, you've got now the format that
Netflix uses or Spotify uses to make

00:07:47.969 --> 00:07:49.530
our library look a bit more like that.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.150
So people can find what
they're looking for.

00:07:51.359 --> 00:07:53.070
Content is key.

00:07:53.549 --> 00:07:56.049
That's, that's my big sort of
take away from the past two years.

00:07:56.369 --> 00:07:58.110
Yeah, I, I, I, I do.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:58.650
It's interesting.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:01.469
You say that the amount of time that
you spend looking for content, because

00:08:01.469 --> 00:08:03.090
it's often the same, the same...

00:08:03.539 --> 00:08:06.390
I find a similar thing happening,
and I think this is, this is an area

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:10.289
where for any, you know, budding
entrepreneurs listening, uh, I think

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:13.289
this is an area where there's still
a great deal of work to be done.

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:23.610
Is it aggregating, aggregating, compelling
content, which is organized by level

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:29.010
in some meaningful way in different
languages into, into, into one place.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:32.930
Because I think there's lots of, it's
easy to find stuff that's, that's,

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:34.560
that's, that's graded by level.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.980
Um, and it's easy to find
stuff that's compelling.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:42.990
It's extremely difficult to find, to
find stuff that is both of those things.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:46.870
And I guess, I guess that's because we
are all such individuals and we're all,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.089
you know, we all have, uh, the, the,
the kinds of information that we'd like

00:08:51.089 --> 00:08:54.390
to surround ourselves with the kind of
types of concept that we like to, that

00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:57.390
we like to, um, to, to, to consume.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:01.349
But I think something, something along
those lines that matches the breadth

00:09:01.349 --> 00:09:04.859
of the whole internet, you know, where
we could do where content could somehow

00:09:04.859 --> 00:09:09.540
be, be bought in from any source and
filtered by interests, filtered by,

00:09:09.599 --> 00:09:11.959
but by level, I, I think that would be.

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:16.120
Uh, yeah, perhaps it's not possible,
perhaps is asking too much, you

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:18.850
know, I still find that 99% of things
I ever try and watch on Netflix,

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:20.260
I turn off within 20 seconds.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:25.300
So maybe it's, it's, it's, it's wishful
thinking, but, but that is that single

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:30.939
source of, um, of, of compelling,
high quality, properly graded language

00:09:30.939 --> 00:09:35.140
content would be certainly for me would
be, uh, a real, um, a real holy grail.

00:09:36.735 --> 00:09:42.615
Yeah, I think that a number of things
I agree with you, and of course, um,

00:09:42.645 --> 00:09:45.375
lots of different people are going to
put out all kinds of different content.

00:09:45.705 --> 00:09:46.965
People's tastes are different.

00:09:47.415 --> 00:09:51.475
And some way of being able to find what
suits your level, tastes, whatever.

00:09:51.885 --> 00:09:55.435
There are ways of making stuff more
accessible, like, uh, you know, at

00:09:55.435 --> 00:09:59.565
LingQ we have the sort of translation
interspersed with the text, so you

00:09:59.565 --> 00:10:01.005
can turn that off or turn that on.

00:10:01.005 --> 00:10:05.865
So even though it might be difficult
for you, even though you might find

00:10:05.865 --> 00:10:09.345
it, you know, you know, like quite
a chore to look up every word,

00:10:09.345 --> 00:10:12.345
but if you've got the translation
interspersed, it kind of helps you along.

00:10:12.765 --> 00:10:15.225
Uh, you can go sentence by
sentence, which helps you along.

00:10:15.495 --> 00:10:19.545
So all of these things, I fact, I
think that link kind of helps you

00:10:19.545 --> 00:10:20.835
to deal with more difficult texts.

00:10:21.155 --> 00:10:23.925
You know, historically people have
said you can't deal with content

00:10:23.925 --> 00:10:25.605
that has more than 5% new words.

00:10:25.605 --> 00:10:30.810
This is Paul Nation of New Zealand was,
you know, n+1 or whatever, trying to

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:34.829
limit the amount of new words there so
that you're constantly able to infer the

00:10:34.829 --> 00:10:36.600
meaning and you can read more comfortably.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.500
And I think that's the big advantage
of being, you know, using a

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.260
digital text and, or a system like
LingQ because you can deal with

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.270
stuff that has 10, 15% new words.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:50.670
Um, the other thing is that,
um, what was I going to say?

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:53.130
Yeah, Netflix obviously is
there's a lot of stuff that's

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:54.629
very silly there on Netflix.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:59.400
But, um, some of these cartoon
texts are, are, are tremendous.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:02.700
And so it's, it's really up to the
individual to find what he wants.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:08.400
And again, we try at LingQ to if people
like certain texts, more than other

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:11.910
texts and this whole idea of relating
it to stuff that you looked at before.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:14.610
So if you're interested in history,
you'll see more history stuff suggested

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:18.720
at you so that you can start to
get closer to people's interests.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.710
Um, we do try to have a thing in there
about, you know, you, you sort of

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:24.420
self-assess yourself at a certain level.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:27.390
So theoretically things at that
level are being suggested to you.

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:35.610
Um, maybe so, um, you know, obviously
someone who said I'm low or intermediate,

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:39.270
they're more likely to find your content,
let's say then, uh, political podcasts.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:45.225
So there are ways that different platforms
can help people find stuff that's at their

00:11:45.225 --> 00:11:46.995
level and at their level of difficulty.

00:11:47.025 --> 00:11:51.615
However, I still think at link we're just
scratching the surface of what's available

00:11:51.615 --> 00:11:52.755
in all these different languages.

00:11:52.855 --> 00:11:55.709
And there's a lot of change coming
down the road as well because Google,

00:11:55.739 --> 00:11:59.699
for example, are now moving into areas
of kind of instantaneous translation.

00:11:59.699 --> 00:12:04.800
So it, so, so they can actually listen
to, and you see this on YouTube with

00:12:04.829 --> 00:12:09.500
multi-lingual subtitles and things like
that, whereby somebody can, you know,

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.930
be talking live, imagine we were doing
this on a live stream and then Google

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:17.880
will be live translating that into,
you know, whatever other language and

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:21.120
that's, I mean, it's, it's still, it's
still not great, but it's a hell of a lot

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:22.860
better than it was even a few years ago.

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:27.540
And so I think there are things
coming down the track in the next and

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:33.390
the next five, 10 years, which, you
know, even if translation, machine

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:36.555
translation never gets to a place
where it's good enough, which I

00:12:36.555 --> 00:12:38.595
think, you know, I I'm skeptical over.

00:12:38.955 --> 00:12:44.265
I think that the, the, the technology
that it will enable for creators around

00:12:44.265 --> 00:12:48.135
the world and people to just start to
make things I think is super exciting.

00:12:48.405 --> 00:12:52.125
And, um, yeah, I'm very, I, you
know, I'm kind of a technology

00:12:52.125 --> 00:12:55.095
skeptic in general when it comes to
language learning, but I've recently

00:12:55.095 --> 00:12:56.475
become a little bit more excited.

00:12:56.475 --> 00:13:00.155
So I'm kind of, I'm interested to see
what the next few years will hold.

00:13:01.585 --> 00:13:04.560
Well, I think as, for example, if you
want to know how to say something in

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.800
your target language, you can write it
all up in, in your native language and

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:11.939
put it on Google Translate and you've
got a pretty good idea of, uh, how

00:13:11.939 --> 00:13:13.410
to say that in the target language.

00:13:13.859 --> 00:13:17.490
Uh, now unfortunately text to
speech is kind of hard to listen to.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:21.510
So I don't think you could be regularly
listening to that kind of stuff.

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:24.719
But if you were trying to zoom
in on a certain kind of, sort of

00:13:24.719 --> 00:13:27.839
terminology, you could write it all
up in your native language, get it

00:13:27.839 --> 00:13:31.860
translated, and you can study that
and acquire those terms, uh, also when

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:36.660
it comes to, um, modern technology,
I use automatic transcription a lot.

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:43.510
I will go into podcasts in Arabic and
in Farsi and I put them on Happyscribe

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:46.980
and I get back like, uh, particularly
in Persian, it's so accurate.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:51.030
The only thing missing is, is good
punctuation, but it's very accurate.

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:53.010
There's the odd word
that doesn't make sense.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:55.470
But 98% of it makes sense.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:14:01.290
So I don't have a transcript for whether
it'd be podcasts in Arabic or in Farsi.

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:03.900
And I just go to happy
trends, happy scribe.

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:07.120
And I have texts and I import
the MP3 file into LingQ.

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:11.010
I import the text into LingQ and I'm
away and I'm learning and, uh, it's it's

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:12.540
events that are happening every day.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.130
So I'm quite familiar with these events.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.960
So there again, I'm using technology.

00:14:18.015 --> 00:14:18.194
Yeah.

00:14:18.525 --> 00:14:22.574
And when I think thinking back to how
things have changed, I bought some books

00:14:22.574 --> 00:14:26.084
